            <div xml:id="d428">

                <pb facs="0810" n="690" sameAs="frus:frus1922v01/pg690"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/4279: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Schurman</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1922-04-02T16:00:00+08:00"><hi rendition="#italic">April 2, 1922—4
                                p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received April 6—1:25
                    p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>127. The following is from Canton: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“April 1, 8 p.m. C. C. Wu informs me it was decided at Mukden, Sun to be
                            President, Tsze [<hi rendition="#italic">Tuan?</hi>] Vice President with
                            elimination of undesirable Anfu element, Government to be formed on
                            federal lines, new Parliament to take the place of old Parliaments,
                            Chang to support Sun with Liang as Premier. Hsu and Wu Pei-fu must
                            accept or be eliminated.” (signed) Huston.<anchor n="24"
                                xml:id="d428-f1"/></p>
                    </quote></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Schurman</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d428-f1" n="24" xml:id="d428-f1-content">
                <p>Jay Calvin Huston, vice consul at Canton.</p>
            </note>
